        "math_academy_id": "q-184612",
        "problem": "Find ${\u22c3}_{i=1}^{3}{X}_{i}$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{X}_{2}={-2,0,2},\\,{X}_{3}={-3,0,3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${\u22c3}_{i=1}^{3}$ means that our index set is \n${1,2,3}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c3}_{i=1}^{3}{X}_{i} & ={X}_{1}\\cup {X}_{2}\\cup {X}_{3} \\\\  & ={-1,0,1}\\,\\cup \\,{-2,0,2}\\,\\cup \\,{-3,0,3} \\\\  & ={0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
